1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,962
Lesson 10.6.

2
00:00:02,343 --> 00:00:05,679
Tanh-linear units on a degree-16 graph.

3
00:00:06,783 --> 00:00:10,960
We already have the pieces: the
tangent a pbit computes, sparse

4
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:15,402
fixed-degree layers and the encoding
of numbers in groups of pbits.

5
00:00:15,782 --> 00:00:19,129
Today we put them together
into the basic unit of a

6
00:00:19,129 --> 00:00:22,607
language model for a pbit
chip: the tanh-linear unit.

7
00:00:22,987 --> 00:00:25,757
And we will see how it
fits in a graph where

8
00:00:25,757 --> 00:00:28,275
each pbit has exactly sixteen neighbors.

9
00:00:29,383 --> 00:00:35,680
A tanh-linear unit
computes tanh(Σ wⱼ xⱼ + b).

10
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:38,867
It is a classical neuron
with tangent activation.

11
00:00:39,247 --> 00:00:43,367
In a pbit, the local field is the
weighted sum of the neighbors'

12
00:00:43,367 --> 00:00:47,550
states, and the mean output is
exactly the tangent of that field.

13
00:00:47,930 --> 00:00:52,198
The tanh-linear unit is therefore
the pbit seen on average.

14
00:00:53,317 --> 00:00:54,180
Now the graph.

15
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:56,613
Each pbit has sixteen neighbors.

16
00:00:56,993 --> 00:01:01,412
If each input is encoded with a
group of four pbits, a unit can

17
00:01:01,412 --> 00:01:05,270
read four inputs: four times
four, sixteen connections.

18
00:01:05,650 --> 00:01:09,264
The four pbits of an input
share the same weight, so

19
00:01:09,264 --> 00:01:12,949
their sum acts as a five-level
probabilistic integer.

20
00:01:14,050 --> 00:01:15,125
There is a subtlety.

21
00:01:15,505 --> 00:01:18,317
The mean output is the
mean of the tangent of the

22
00:01:18,317 --> 00:01:21,015
noisy field, not the
tangent of the mean field.

23
00:01:21,395 --> 00:01:25,375
Since the tangent is curved, the
noise of the inputs produces a bias.

24
00:01:25,755 --> 00:01:32,948
We measure it with four thousand random
units: with one pbit per input, the

25
00:01:32,948 --> 00:01:39,662
root-mean-square bias is 0.197; with
four, 0.085; with sixteen, 0.036.

26
00:01:40,783 --> 00:01:43,642
With a fixed degree of sixteen, more pbits

27
00:01:43,642 --> 00:01:46,228
per input means fewer inputs per unit.

28
00:01:46,608 --> 00:01:50,850
Fewer inputs forces more layers
to mix all the information: for a

29
00:01:50,850 --> 00:01:55,157
dimension of one thousand twenty-four,
with fan-in sixteen two and

30
00:01:55,157 --> 00:01:58,615
a half layers suffice; with
fan-in four, five layers.

31
00:01:58,995 --> 00:02:03,931
Four by four is a compromise:
moderate bias, reasonable depth.

32
00:02:04,311 --> 00:02:08,130
Moreover, training with noise
absorbs much of the bias.

33
00:02:09,250 --> 00:02:10,880
This is how tokens enter the chip.

34
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:14,918
Each token's embedding
vector, computed digitally,

35
00:02:14,918 --> 00:02:18,210
is written as biases on
groups of four pbits.

36
00:02:18,590 --> 00:02:21,372
From there, tanh-linear
layers with fan-in four

37
00:02:21,372 --> 00:02:24,272
project and mix the
information inside the graph.

38
00:02:24,652 --> 00:02:28,235
The output is read by averaging
samples, as in the previous

39
00:02:28,235 --> 00:02:31,576
lesson, and returns to digital
for the vocabulary head.

40
00:02:32,683 --> 00:02:38,939
In practice: use tangent in the MLP and
DyT as normalization, mask the projections

41
00:02:38,939 --> 00:02:44,431
to fan-in four with groups of four
pbits, and train with sampled passes.

42
00:02:44,811 --> 00:02:49,506
In lab twenty-nine you will convert
a small model and measure the price.

43
00:02:50,617 --> 00:02:55,503
Common mistakes: assuming the mean of
the tangent is the tangent of the mean.

44
00:02:55,883 --> 00:02:59,420
Spending the sixteen connections
on single-pbit inputs.

45
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:02,852
Forgetting that a small
fan-in costs depth.

46
00:03:03,232 --> 00:03:07,113
And training deterministically
to deploy stochastically.

47
00:03:08,217 --> 00:03:12,378
In summary, the tanh-linear unit
is the pbit seen on average.

48
00:03:12,758 --> 00:03:18,373
In a degree-sixteen graph, four inputs
times four pbits balance the noise

49
00:03:18,373 --> 00:03:23,143
bias, which we measured at 0.085,
and the depth needed to mix.

50
00:03:23,523 --> 00:03:27,372
Embeddings enter as biases and
the output is read by averaging.

51
00:03:28,483 --> 00:03:33,282
To practice: explain why a pbit
implements a tanh-linear unit.

52
00:03:33,662 --> 00:03:36,341
Count the levels of the sum of four pbits.

53
00:03:36,721 --> 00:03:40,606
Compute the layers to mix a dimension
of four thousand ninety-six.

54
00:03:40,986 --> 00:03:42,810
And explain the origin of the bias.
